  {
    "_ulid": "01KJ62NKTRBZZVSW0Y8DAC09YY",
    "slugs": [
      "task-iteration-boundary-counting"
    ],
    "title": "Implement index-based iteration boundary counting in computeIterationSummaries",
    "type": "task",
    "spec_ref": "@session-log-show",
    "plan_ref": "@plan-sequence-based-iteration-counting-for-session-anal",
    "status": "completed",
    "blocked_by": [],
    "closed_reason": "Merged in PR #486. Implemented index-based iteration boundary counting using prompt.sent events, with legacy fallback for older sessions.",
    "depends_on": [],
    "context": [],
    "priority": 2,
    "tags": [
      "cli",
      "bug"
    ],
    "vcs_refs": [],
    "created_at": "2026-02-23T20:22:21.784Z",
    "started_at": "2026-02-23T22:31:18.626Z",
    "completed_at": "2026-02-23T23:02:12.592Z",
    "notes": [
      {
        "_ulid": "01KJ62NKTRCQXDK9YJ6GJ9H337",
        "created_at": "2026-02-23T20:22:21.784Z",
        "author": "@claude",
        "content": "Rewrite computeIterationSummaries() in src/sessions/store.ts to use\nprompt.sent event positions as iteration boundaries instead of relying\non the data.iteration field (which suffers from a closure capture race\nin ralph's fire-and-forget event logging).\n"
      },
      {
        "_ulid": "01KJ62QHSE8CMYZV49CCT9QNYM",
        "created_at": "2026-02-23T20:23:25.230Z",
        "author": "@claude",
        "content": "## Implementation Notes\n\n### Problem\ncomputeIterationSummaries() in src/sessions/store.ts:718-802 groups events by data.iteration field. Ralph's fire-and-forget appendEvent() call at src/cli/commands/ralph.ts:1573-1579 captures the loop iteration variable by closure reference. By the time the async callback executes, iteration has advanced, so streaming events get tagged with wrong iteration numbers. Result: kspec session log show reports most iterations as \"4 events\" when they had hundreds.\n\nA separate task (@01KJ5WV0) fixes the capture-by-value bug in ralph.ts. This task makes the counting system resilient to producer-side data errors.\n\n### Approach: Index-Based Boundary Splitting\nRalph emits awaited prompt.sent events with { phase: \"task-work\", iteration: N } at ralph.ts:1463 at the start of each iteration. Use these as authoritative iteration boundaries.\n\nCRITICAL: Use array indices from readEvents() output, NOT seq values. appendEvent() is explicitly not concurrency-safe (store.ts:260) — concurrent fire-and-forget calls can produce duplicate seq numbers. Array indices provide deterministic splitting immune to seq races.\n\n### Algorithm\n1. Read all events via readEvents() (returns sorted array)\n2. Find boundary events: type === \"prompt.sent\" AND data?.phase === \"task-work\" AND typeof data?.iteration === \"number\" — record their array index and iteration number\n3. Validate: filter out non-monotonic iteration numbers, deduplicate\n4. If no valid boundaries → legacy fallback (current data.iteration grouping, extracted into helper function)\n5. Build index ranges: [0, boundary1.idx), [boundary1.idx, boundary2.idx), etc.\n6. Pre-boundary events merge into first iteration\n7. Extract tasks_started/tasks_completed per iteration (unchanged logic)\n\n### Files to Modify\nsrc/sessions/store.ts:\n- Rewrite computeIterationSummaries() (lines 718-802) with boundary algorithm\n- Extract current logic into legacyIterationGrouping() helper for fallback\n- Update getSessionLogDetail() (~line 820) to derive iteration_count from summaries.length instead of calling countIterations() separately\n- Add countIterationsBoundaryAware() — lightweight boundary counter for getSessionLogSummary() (used by session log list and session log stats) to prevent iteration_count disagreements between list and show views\n\ntests/session-log-show.test.ts:\n- Update existing fixtures (~line 171) to include phase: \"task-work\" field\n- Add tests:\n  1. Multi-iteration with prompt.sent boundaries (index-based grouping)\n  2. Wrong data.iteration on streaming events (still grouped correctly)\n  3. Legacy fallback — no prompt.sent events with phase field\n  4. Malformed boundaries — prompt.sent with missing phase/iteration (graceful fallback)\n  5. Single iteration — one boundary captures all events\n  6. Pre-loop events — session.start before first prompt.sent merged into iteration 1\n  7. Mixed phase prompt.sent — both task-work and reflect present, only task-work used\n  8. Duplicate seq values — concurrent events with same seq, boundary math correct\n\n### Key Implementation Details\n- SessionEvent.data is typed as unknown (types.ts:105) — treat boundary data as untrusted, validate phase and iteration fields before use\n- readEvents() at store.ts:320-340 sorts by seq — index order follows seq order\n- Reflect prompt.sent uses phase: \"reflect\" (ralph.ts:1648) — safely excluded\n- Subagents and wrap-up agents don't emit prompt.sent to parent session (confirmed in src/ralph/subagent.ts:229)\n- No changes needed to types.ts, ralph.ts, or session.ts\n\n### Verification\n1. npx vitest run tests/session-log-show.test.ts\n2. npm run dev -- session log show 01KJ4VNM — verify accurate counts across all iterations (not \"4 events\" for most)\n3. Cross-check: session log list iteration counts match session log show\n4. Full suite: npx vitest run",
        "supersedes": null
      },
      {
        "_ulid": "01KJ6ABC15HHM9PSNPMETWRDFN",
        "created_at": "2026-02-23T22:36:34.725Z",
        "author": "@claude",
        "content": "Implemented index-based iteration boundary counting. computeIterationSummaries() now finds prompt.sent events with phase: 'task-work' as boundaries, splits events by array index ranges. Legacy fallback preserved for sessions without boundaries. Added countIterationsBoundaryAware() for getSessionLogSummary() consistency. getSessionLogDetail() now derives iteration_count from summaries.length. 8 new test cases covering: boundary grouping, wrong data.iteration resilience, legacy fallback, pre-boundary merging, single iteration, mixed phases, malformed boundaries, duplicate seq values. All 35 session-log-show tests pass.",
        "supersedes": null
      }
    ],
    "todos": [],
    "automation": "eligible",
    "_sourceFile": "/workspace/.kspec/project.tasks.yaml"
  },
  {
    "_ulid": "01KJ65H0N4F3TK1A9EZRSZAHBT",
    "slugs": [],
    "title": "Design native task-limit enforcement for ralph agent iterations",
    "type": "spike",
    "description": "## Problem\n\nRalph loop agents are not stopping after completing one task per iteration. Evidence from session 01KJ5WZPNDYY68ZX143D06FXCB shows an agent submitted task @01KJ4SMA, then immediately started and completed task @01KJ5WV0 with zero ralph intervention between them. The maxTasks: 1 setting was never enforced.\n\nTwo root causes:\n\n1. **No event-to-agent tracking**: Ralph has no way to attribute a `kspec task submit` or `kspec task complete` to the currently active agent iteration. The marker file approach is passive — ralph writes it, but only when it detects a complete command in the event stream. Since the agent used `submit` (not `complete`), the marker was never written.\n\n2. **Submit not treated as completion**: `detectTaskCompleteCommand()` in ralph.ts (line 229) explicitly excludes `kspec task submit`. But submit means the agent's work is done — a review agent should take over. Submit should count the same as complete for task-limit purposes.\n\n3. **Shell script hook is fragile**: The current `ralph-task-limit-guard.sh` is a shell script that parses JSON with jq, walks directories to find project root, and reads a marker file. This should be a native kspec command (like `kspec session checkpoint` is for the Stop hook) for reliability and testability.\n\n## Investigation Goals\n\n- How can ralph reliably track which events belong to the active agent? Options: session scoping, event correlation IDs, agent turn boundaries\n- What's the right enforcement point? PreToolUse hook blocking `task start`, or injecting a stop signal, or both?\n- Should the hook be `kspec ralph guard` or similar native command that reads ralph state directly instead of a marker file?\n\n## Design Deliverables\n\n- Clear design for native task-limit hook (replacing ralph-task-limit-guard.sh)\n- Updated detection logic: submit counts as completion for limit purposes\n- Event attribution approach so ralph knows what the active agent did\n- Spec updates if the current @ralph-task-limit spec needs changes\n- New task(s) derived from the design for implementation work\n\n## Key Files\n\n- src/cli/commands/ralph.ts (lines 227-231 detectTaskCompleteCommand, 1520-1557 limit counting, 1314-1773 main loop)\n- .claude/hooks/ralph-task-limit-guard.sh (current shell script hook)\n- src/cli/commands/setup.ts (hook installation, GUARD_SCRIPTS)\n- src/cli/commands/session.ts (session checkpoint as model for native hook)\n- .kspec/modules/cli.yaml (@ralph-task-limit spec)\n- templates/agents-sections/06-ralph-loop.md (agent instructions)\n- .claude/skills/task-work/SKILL.md (agent stop instructions)",
    "spec_ref": null,
    "meta_ref": null,
    "plan_ref": null,
    "status": "completed",
    "blocked_by": [],
    "closed_reason": "Spike complete. Investigated session scoping approach for kspec, including:\n- Cross-harness env var research (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Gemini CLI)\n- Current session/ralph/hook architecture analysis\n- Design of session-scoped budget enforcement replacing marker files + bash hooks\n- Dual review (internal + Codex) with all MUST-FIX findings addressed\n\nDeliverables:\n- Plan @plan-session-scoping-for-kspec imported with 6 specs, 10 tasks\n- Dependencies set between tasks\n- Specs have traits: @trait-json-output, @trait-semantic-exit-codes, @trait-error-guidance\n- Key design decisions: budget on local filesystem (not shadow branch), single-writer guarantee, cooperative enforcement, end-loop migrated to session state, sessions always closed",
    "depends_on": [],
    "context": [],
    "priority": 1,
    "tags": [
      "ralph",
      "hooks",
      "design"
    ],
    "vcs_refs": [],
    "created_at": "2026-02-23T21:12:16.803Z",
    "started_at": "2026-02-24T00:42:18.887Z",
    "completed_at": "2026-02-24T01:19:28.006Z",
    "notes": [
      {
        "_ulid": "01KJ6JZEJADFS9RJ2EVEW27WWQ",
        "created_at": "2026-02-24T01:07:21.290Z",
        "author": "@claude",
        "content": "## Session Progress — Spike Investigation Complete, Plan Drafted\n\n### What was done\n1. **Full investigation of session scoping approach** — explored current session infrastructure, ralph loop, hooks, task schema, and environment variable handling across 4 agent harnesses (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Gemini CLI).\n\n2. **Design decisions made with user:**\n   - Soft advisory task claiming (not hard isolation)\n   - Budget enforcement built into kspec task start itself (no hook needed for task limiting)\n   - Session state machine: ralph unlocks → agent starts task → agent submits → kspec auto-locks → ralph resets for next iteration\n   - Single-writer guarantee for atomicity (ralph and agent are never concurrent)\n   - KSPEC_SESSION_ID env var flows through agent tool calls\n\n3. **Plan written in kspec import format** at `/home/chapel/.claude/plans/bright-seeking-marble.md`:\n   - 5 specs: session-creation-and-env-injection, session-scoped-task-claiming, task-budget-enforcement, native-guard-commands, ralph-session-budget-integration\n   - 9 tasks covering schema changes, CLI commands, budget enforcement, native guards, ralph simplification, multi-harness env injection\n   - Dry run passes clean: `kspec plan import --module cli --dry-run`\n\n4. **Dual review completed** — both an internal agent review and Codex review-plan found convergent issues. Reviews saved at `/tmp/codex-plan-review.md`.\n\n### Review findings to address before import\nMUST-FIX:\n- Add ACs for invalid/stale KSPEC_SESSION_ID (session not found, malformed ID)\n- Add migration cleanup ACs (remove old hook entries from settings.json, delete old bash scripts)\n- Add cross-file consistency ACs (task state + budget state divergence on partial failure)\n- Fix `ac-cleanup` to be concrete and testable\n\nSHOULD-FIX (under discussion with user):\n- Shadow branch contention: should budget state live in local file vs shadow branch?\n- Submit vs complete with review subagent: should subagent inherit session ID?\n- End-loop marker: leave as-is or migrate to session state?\n- Wrap-up message: error message should contain wrap-up instructions\n- Explicitly state interactive sessions have no budget\n- Fix file reference: `tasks ready` is in tasks.ts not task.ts\n- State explicitly that enforcement is cooperative-only\n\n### What's left\n- User wants to discuss specific findings (shadow branch contention, subagent session inheritance, end-loop marker, wrap-up message) before updating the plan\n- After discussion: update plan with fixes, re-run dry run, import\n- Then: derive implementation tasks from the plan\n\n### Key files\n- Plan: /home/chapel/.claude/plans/bright-seeking-marble.md\n- Codex review: /tmp/codex-plan-review.md\n- Internal review: in conversation context",
        "supersedes": null
      },
      {
